# Stop the service

The walkthrough's `main.ts` already registers the handler before starting
`service.wait()`, and stops the service when its shutdown signal arrives:

```ts
Deno.addSignalListener("SIGINT", () => void service.stop());
Deno.addSignalListener("SIGTERM", () => void service.stop());
await service.wait();
```

These signal APIs are a Deno example. A Node process, supervisor, or embedded
host can trigger the same `service.stop()` lifecycle through its own shutdown
mechanism. If your application starts additional timers, database pools, or
background tasks, close those as well. Trellis does not own those resources.
Avoid forcing process exit before shutdown has completed.

For tests, close clients and stop the service in `finally`; always stop the test
runtime even when an assertion fails. The
[checked-in test](https://github.com/qlever-llc/trellis/blob/main/docs/examples/orders/service_test.ts)
shows this sequence.

Rust uses its generated service `run()` lifecycle rather than TypeScript's
`wait()` API. Follow the [Rust library guide](/guides/libraries/rust) for the
current generated facade. Application-created tasks outside that lifecycle need
their own shutdown handling.
